---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { CATEGORIES, STATUS } from "../lib/capabilities.mjs";

const security = CATEGORIES.find((category) => category.id === "security");
const compliance = CATEGORIES.find((category) => category.id === "compliance");
const sections = [security, compliance];
---

<Page
  title="Security — Shared Auth"
  description="How shared-auth separates credential lanes, revokes access, and refuses to hold what it does not need: no biometric templates, no implicit trust, machine-checked capability claims."
  eyebrow="Security posture"
  heading="Fail closed, hold less, and publish what is actually enabled."
  lede="Most identity incidents are not cryptographic failures. They are a credential accepted somewhere it should not have been, or a revocation that did not take effect. These are the properties we design against."
>
  <section id="principles">
    <div class="grid-3">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Two credential lanes that never meet</h3>
        <p>
          A service credential is accepted only on introspection. An end-user
          token is accepted only on user operations. Presenting either one on
          the other lane is refused, not merely ignored.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>No biometric custody</h3>
        <p>
          Passkey ceremonies relay opaque credential JSON. The platform
          authenticator stays the biometric boundary; no fingerprint or face
          template is ever accepted, stored or forwarded.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Authentication is not authorization</h3>
        <p>
          The identity plane proves who a principal is and to what assurance
          level. Tenant, role and resource authorization stay with the
          application that owns the resource.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Revocation that actually fences</h3>
        <p>
          Per-session revocation, per-principal epoch and not-before fences, and
          an estate-wide stop under dual control. A revoked principal stops
          being accepted, not eventually, but at the next verification.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Capability claims are machine-checked</h3>
        <p>
          The server publishes what it will actually do. A method that is
          disabled in a deployment reports as disabled instead of being quietly
          claimed in marketing copy.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Self-hosted by construction</h3>
        <p>
          The deployment chooses the region, the database and the key custody.
          Data residency is not a plan tier here; it is where you decided to run
          the container.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  {
    sections.map((category) => (
      <section id={category.id}>
        <h2>{category.name}</h2>
        <p class="lede">{category.blurb}</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Control</th>
                <th>Status</th>
                <th>Detail</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {category.capabilities.map((capability) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{capability.title}</td>
                  <td>
                    <span class={`status ${STATUS[capability.status].className}`}>
                      {STATUS[capability.status].label}
                    </span>
                  </td>
                  <td>{capability.note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }
  <section id="reporting" class="panel">
    <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Report suspected vulnerabilities through the security policy published in
      each repository rather than in a public issue. Please include the affected
      hostname or repository, the request shape, and what you expected to be
      refused.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a
        class="primary"
        href="https://github.com/shared-auth/shared-auth.github.io/blob/main/SECURITY.md"
        >Security policy ↗</a
      ><a href="/features/">Capability register</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
